"A cannon on the ground. An angle you dial in. A power number that
scales your shot. Pull the trigger — an arc rises against the sky,
peaks, and falls back down. If your aim is true, the shell lands on the
target. If not, you turn a small knob and try again.

That is the projectile family. Artillery games. Angry-birds-shape
physics puzzles. Trebuchet toys on the phone in the airport gate. Every
one of them is `phys/kinematics` — the equation of motion for constant
acceleration — with a paint call each frame.

This chapter builds the whole family from one primitive: a cannon that
spawns a ball with initial velocity `(cos angle * power, sin angle *
power)` and lets gravity do the rest. Then we compare the ball's actual
path to the closed-form kinematics equation and prove they agree. Then
we knock over three cans.

---

## 4.1 The cannon

Aim, fire, watch the arc.

```scheme
;; Turn an aim angle (degrees) and a power into a launch velocity vec.
;; 0° = flat right, 90° = straight up. In screen space y grows DOWN, so
;; 'up' on the trajectory is y-negative — hence the sign flip on vy.
(define pi 3.14159)
(define (deg->rad d) (* d (/ pi 180)))

(define (launch-vel power deg)
  (let ((r (deg->rad deg)))
    (vec/make (* power (geom/cos r))
              (* -1 power (geom/sin r)))))

;; A 45° shot at power 6: equal rightward and upward speed.
(launch-vel 6 45)
;; => (4.242643501675357 -4.242637872561345)
```

Note the sign flip on the y-component. In screen space y grows down, but
'up' on a real trajectory is y-negative. `(* -1 power (geom/sin r))`
gives us a launch that goes UP the screen — you can see it as the
negative second component above.

Note `let`: `r` is computed once and both components use it. On a real
cabinet you would dial the angle with up/down (clamped to 1–89°) and fire
on button 'a', spawning a shell with this velocity. Play with `power = 4`
(short range) and `power = 10` (over-the-horizon).

---

## 4.2 The range formula

Physics 101: for a projectile launched with speed `v` at angle `a` on
flat ground under gravity `g`, the range is `v*v * sin(2a) / g`.
Maximum range at `a = 45 degrees`. Range zero at 0 or 90.

Let's verify. We spawn a shell, wait for it to land, and measure how far
it went. Then compare to the formula.

```scheme
;; Position at time t. Each axis is `phys/kinematics x0 v0 a t`, which
;; returns (position velocity); we take the position (car).
(define (shell-at x0 y0 vx vy g t)
  (vec/make (car (phys/kinematics x0 vx 0 t))
            (car (phys/kinematics y0 vy g t))))

;; A shot fired at launch height returns to it at t = 2·|vy|/g.
(define (flight-time power deg g)
  (/ (* -2 (cadr (launch-vel power deg))) g))

;; Fire, land, measure the horizontal distance; compare to the formula.
(define (verify-range power deg g x0 y0)
  (let* ((v         (launch-vel power deg))
         (t         (flight-time power deg g))
         (landing   (shell-at x0 y0 (car v) (cadr v) g t))
         (actual    (- (car landing) x0))
         (r         (deg->rad deg))
         (predicted (/ (* power power (* 2 (geom/sin r) (geom/cos r))) g)))
    (list actual predicted)))

(verify-range 8 45 0.3 30 210)
;; => (213.33333333314556 213.33333333314556)
```

We are RUNNING the physics and CHECKING it against the theory — and they
agree to the last digit, because `phys/kinematics` IS the closed-form
equation of motion. The range formula `v²·sin(2a)/g` and the simulated
landing point are two views of the same equation. That is the
pedagogical superpower of a physics-first engine: the code does not just
LOOK physical, it IS. When kids get to trigonometry in school, they have
already touched sin and cos. When they get to integration, they have
already accumulated velocity into position.

---

## 4.3 Draw the trajectory

We can visualize the arc by remembering every position the shell
occupies and painting them all.

```scheme
;; The trail is just the shell's position sampled at a series of times.
;; `map` over the time samples builds the whole arc as a list of points.
(define (trail power deg g x0 y0 step count)
  (let ((v (launch-vel power deg)))
    (map (lambda (i)
           (shell-at x0 y0 (car v) (cadr v) g (* i step)))
         (iota count))))

;; A 55° shot, sampled every 5 frames, first 4 points.
(trail 8 55 0.3 30 210 5 4)
;; => ((30 210) (52.94308402139936 180.9839368311204) (75.88616804279872 159.46787366224075) (98.82925206419807 145.45181049336114))
```

Each point is `(shell-at ... t)` for a different `t`. The x-values climb
steadily (constant horizontal speed) while the y-values fall by less and
less then more and more — the parabola. Paint these points and the kid
literally SEES the arc curve under gravity.

Because `trail` is a pure `map` over sample times, there is no unbounded
memory to worry about: you ask for exactly `count` points. Want a denser
arc? Lower `step`. Want the whole flight? Set `count` to cover
`flight-time`.

---

## 4.4 Target practice

Now put three cans on the floor. Knock them over.

```scheme
;; Three cans down-range at x = 200, 240, 280. A shot hits a can if its
;; arc passes within 8px of the can's x while near floor height.
(define (hits-can? power deg g x0 y0 cx)
  (let ((v (launch-vel power deg)))
    (let loop ((t 1))
      (let ((p (shell-at x0 y0 (car v) (cadr v) g t)))
        (cond ((>= (cadr p) y0)                  ;; landed back at floor
               (< (abs (- (car p) cx)) 8))       ;; near this can?
              ((> t 500) #f)
              (else (loop (+ t 1))))))))

;; Which of the three cans does a 45° / power-8 shot knock over?
;; It lands at x≈245, so it drops onto can2 (x=240) and misses the others.
(define cans '(200 240 280))
(map (lambda (cx) (hits-can? 8 45 0.3 30 210 cx)) cans)
;; => (#f #t #f)
```

`hits-can?` walks the arc a frame at a time until the shell descends back
to floor height, then asks one plain question: is its landing x within
8px of the can's x? Adjust `power` and `deg` to walk the landing point
onto a different can — that is aiming.

On a real cabinet a hit would trigger a camera-shake for impact feedback
— Chapter 6 covers that as FEEL. Here the hit test is the whole game:
composition of `launch-vel`, `shell-at`, and a distance check.

---

## 4.5 The four patterns

**Composition.** A projectile is composed from `launch-vel` (angle +
power → velocity) and `shell-at` (a `phys/kinematics` call per axis). A
cannon is composed of aim state (angle) and power. The target is just a
can's x-coordinate.

**Orchestration.** The frame loop orchestrates poll (angle adjustment),
poll (fire → build a launch velocity), physics evaluation (`shell-at` at
the current time), hit test (`hits-can?`). Compute the velocity BEFORE
you evaluate position, or the shell has no motion this frame.

**In-routing.** Angle-adjust routes to a new angle; fire routes to a new
shell velocity. Two inputs, two small functions, cleanly separated.

**Routing.** Hit results route through `hits-can?` per can. A hit routes
to a score increment (and a shake, for feel). If we added shell-and-shell
(bullets deflecting bullets), it would be another distance test.

---

## 4.6 Common desktop tasks

**Balloon-pop unlock.** A shop hides discounts inside virtual
balloons floating across the item page. Customer aims a cannon
(one input = aim, one input = fire) and pops balloons. Each popped
balloon reveals a discount code. Uses this chapter's `launch-vel` +
`shell-at` + a `hits-can?`-style distance test against each balloon's
position.

**Delivery-arc animation.** After purchase, a small package projectile
arcs from the shop's icon to the shopper's cart icon, following
`phys/kinematics`. The physics IS the delivery-in-progress feedback.
Runs in about 1.5 seconds — enough for the kid to notice and smile.

---

## 4.7 Pitfalls

**Shells skip over the cans.** Your `hits-can?` samples time in steps of
1 but the shell moves so fast it jumps past the can's x between samples.
Sample at finer `t`, or widen the 8px window.

**A shot hits cans it flew over.** Your loop did not stop at the floor,
so the arc kept descending forever and eventually crossed every can's x
at some low y. Stop the walk when `(cadr p)` reaches `y0`, as above.

**Range formula disagrees with actual.** They should agree exactly here,
because `phys/kinematics` IS the closed form and `flight-time` is
analytic. If they differ, check your sign on `vy` or your `deg->rad`.

**Trajectory list is huge.** `trail` gives you exactly `count` points —
ask for fewer, or a larger `step`.

**Cannon fires below the floor.** Check that the launch height `y0`
(210) is above the floor line. If you launch AT the floor, the shell
lands on frame one.

---

## 4.8 Try this yourself

1. Add wind: give the x-axis a small acceleration in `shell-at` (pass a
   nonzero `a` to the x `phys/kinematics`). The shell drifts down-range
   and the simple range formula no longer holds — the sim still does.
2. Launch the cannon from a platform (`y0 = 100`). The range equation
   gains a term for launch height. Derive it, or just measure with
   `verify-range`.
3. Make a target move: give the can x a velocity and evaluate its
   position at landing time. Now you must lead the shot.
4. Fire a spread: `map` `hits-can?` over angles `deg-5`, `deg`, `deg+5`.
   Shotgun.
5. Compute the predicted landing x each frame (evaluate `shell-at` at
   `flight-time`) and record it as an aim marker. The kid learns aim
   visually.
6. Let a shell continue past the floor with a bounce (flip vy × 0.6 at
   `y0`) and give cans a health value. Now one shell may clip several
   cans as it skips.

---

## 4.9 What we built

A cannon, a projectile, an arc, a target, and a hit-counter. The
range formula, verified in-sim. The trail visualization. Camera-shake
as feedback.

Next chapter: turn the cannon vertical, put the player at the bottom,
enemies at the top. That is the shoot-em-up, and it is where entity
pooling and collision layers earn their keep.")

